"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\nbrowsewrap agreement turns on whether the Web site puts a reasonably prudent user on inquiry\nnotice of the terms of the contract ... [and] to establish the enforceability of a browsewrap\nagreement, a textual notice should be required to advise consumers that continued use of a Web\nsite will constitute the consumer's agreement to be bound by the Web site's terms of use.\").\nKauders cites many of the federal decisions (applying state law) discussed in these Reporters'\nNotes. When evaluating whether a particular transaction satisfies these requirements, the courts\nlook at a range of factors (see those listed in Comment 2) and engage in a \"fact-intensive\"\nevaluation of the \"totality of the circumstances.\" See, e.g., Kauders v. Uber Techs., Inc., 159\nN.E.3d 1033 (Mass. 2021); Zachman v. Hudson Valley Fed. Credit Union, 49 F.4th 95 (2d Cir.\n2022) (applying New York law, whether terms and conditions were incorporated into a \"web-\nbased agreement\" includes a review of the \"design and content\" of the interface to determine if the\nterms and conditions were \"clearly identified\" and \"accessible\" to the consumer); Archer v.\nGrubhub, Inc., 190 N.E.3d 1024 (Mass. 2022) (applying Kauders' \"totality of the circumstances\"\ntest to determine if consumer has \"reasonable notice\" of the terms of the agreement); Nancy S.\nKim, Digital Contracts, 75 Bus. LAW. 1682 (2019-2020) (\"[C]ourts ... assess the reasonableness\nof notice by evaluating the website experience from the perspective of the offeree and by\nconsidering how the drafter chose to present terms.\"); Oberstein v. Live Nation Ent., Inc., 60 F.4th\n505 (9th Cir. 2023) (applying Kauders and the \"totality of the circumstances\" test and noting that\n\"while the fact-intensive inquiry required under the two-part reasonableness test may lead to\ndifferent results as courts [in different states] encounter novel fact patterns, the general legal\nframework remains unchanged\"); Nancy S. Kim, Developments in Digital \"Wrap\" Contracts, 77\nBus. LAW. 275 (2021-2022) (courts focus \"on the nature of the transaction and the totality of the\nuser's interaction with the company\" and \"take into account the nature of the relationship between\nthe parties and the type of dispute\"); David McGowan, Consumer Contracts and the Restatement\nProject, San Diego Legal Studs. Paper No. 19-424 (2019) (manuscript at 6), https://papers.ssrn.\ncom/sol3/papers.cfm?abstractid=3534269 (\"The key principles under the Seventh Circuit's\nIllinois-based approach are simple and entirely consistent with California, New York, and the\nNinth and Second Circuits: (i) does the consumer receive reasonable notice that they are agreeing\nto the terms of a contract; and (ii) does the consumer receive reasonable notice of the terms and\nconditions of that contract.\") (footnote omitted). See, e.g., Emmanuel v. Handy Techs., Inc., 992\nF.3d 1 (1st Cir. 2021) (engaging in a fact-intensive evaluation and concluding that the consumers\nhad \"reasonable notice of the term in the Agreement\"); Theodore v. Uber Techs., Inc., 442 F. Supp.\n3d 433 (D. Mass. 2020) (applying a two-part test in which the court had first to establish whether\nthe terms were \"reasonably communicated to the [consumers],\" followed by an inquiry on whether\nthe \"the record shows that those terms were 'accepted and, if so, the manner of acceptance\"')\n(quoting Cullinane v. Uber Techs., Inc., 893 F.3d 53, 62 (1st Cir. 2018)); Scotti v. Tough Mudder\nInc., 97 N.Y.S.3d 825, 855 (Sup. Ct. 2019) (concluding that notice of the arbitration provision was\nnot reasonably conspicuous, and therefore the arbitration term was not adopted, and noting that\ncourts ought to look at the \"design and content of the relevant interface to determine if the contract\nterms were presented to the offeree in a way that would put her [or him] on inquiry notice of such\n47\n@§2\n"